# tool-stamps.pin — what each shipped cargo tool was, at the release that last shipped it.
#
#   <tool> <version> <source stamp>
#
# WRITTEN BY `slopdesk-release bump-tools`, read by it and by `slopdesk-release stamps --check`.
# Never hand-edit: the stamp is a digest of the tool's source closure, so a value typed here that
# no tree produces makes every future run report the tool as changed, forever.
#
# THE VERSION IS THE IDENTITY, NOT THE SHA. The release tarball's `MANIFEST.json` carries these
# versions, and the install side restarts a daemon when its version moved. It cannot compare the
# shipped BYTES instead: `slopdesk-release package` signs every binary with `--timestamp`, so a
# rebuild-and-resign of an unchanged tool produces a different file every time. The stamp is what
# makes the version honest — a version may only move when the stamp did — and the version is what
# survives signing.
#
# SEEDED at product v0.4.0 with the versions the crates already declared, and RE-SEEDED when the
# pipeline moved from shell to `slopdesk-release`: the digest is now over repo-RELATIVE paths, so it
# is a property of the tree rather than of where the tree was checked out. No version moved, because
# no source did. Every tool reads "same" against this seed; they diverge from here.
#
# slopdesk-videohostd was SEEDED the same way on 2026-09-02, the day it joined the tarball — the
# line below is `slopdesk-release stamps`' own output for that tree, so it reads "same" until its
# source moves and `bump-tools` moves it from 0.1.0 on the first release that changes it.
slopdesk-ctl 0.1.0 cc8d08e182787ef99b24872d871c2138987a0f27bcb7e230e6831ed6076e531e
